BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tyler Reeve & Django Walker
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T190000
UID:260910nrA5nl7PWkCFIqvviiMQ-w
DESCRIPTION:Born in Mobile\, Alabama\, and educated \\(socially at the ver
 y least\\) at The Florida State University\, Tyler’s down home sensibili
 ty\, attitude and humor come across in his songs and through his live perf
 ormances.  Always a storyteller\, songwriting fits quite naturally for Ree
 ve.  “I’m a song guy\; I love songs\, I love stories\, I love the ride
  music can take you on.”  Tyler’s performance are riddled with stories
  – usually funny stories – about inspiration for his songs\, having a 
 few too many\, and even spending a night or two in jail.  \n\n	Reeve has h
 ad his hand in writing some of country music’s biggest songs of the last
  few years\, including the 2018 BMI Song of The Year\, “In Case You Didn
 ’t Know” \\(Brett Young\\)\, which has streamed over 1 Billion times. 
 He also had a multi-week \\#1 & XM The Highway’s 2020 Song of The Year\,
  and ACM Musical Event of The Year nom\, “Does To Me” \\(Luke Combs fe
 aturing Eric Church\\)\, and recent Highway \\#1 Mississippi Or Me \\(Rile
 y Green\\).  Other notables include “Whiskey On My Breath” \\(Love & T
 heft\\)\, “Where I Go When I Drink” \\(Chris Young\\)\, White Girls\, 
 White Wine & Adderall \\(Kid Rock\\)\, How Ya Doin Up There \\(Scotty McCr
 eery\\) and “I Got Away With You” \\(Luke Combs\\).
LOCATION:Carneros Resort and Spa
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/108736158?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=28
 7&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:USAF Commanders Jazz Ensemble
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T193000
UID:260909hpBNyj4Ie0uNPX93Bdxfgw
DESCRIPTION:**Free admission\, but ticket required**. Maximum 4 per person
 .\n\nWith a diverse repertoire and exceptional talent\, the **Commanders J
 azz Ensemble** offers far more than traditional big band music. While the
 y honor legends like Duke Ellington\, Count Basie\, and Glenn Miller\, the
 y seamlessly transition into cool jazz\, bebop\, Latin rhythms\, Broadway 
 hits\, and patriotic music.\n\nThe ensemble is part of the **United States
  Air Force Band of the Golden West**\, stationed in the San Francisco Bay 
 Area at Travis Air Force Base\, California. The only active duty Air Force
  band west of the Rockies\, the Band of the Golden West is comprised of ab
 out 60 talented and versatile Airmen-musicians under the command of Captai
 n Brian O’Donnell. In addition to performing for civilian communities th
 roughout the states of California\, Washington\, Oregon\, Arizona\, and Ne
 vada\, the band supports 13 Air Force Bases\, 8 Air Force Reserve Wings\, 
 and 6 recruiting squadrons in over 250 annual performances for 1.5 million
  listeners.\n\nThe organization offers several outstanding performing grou
 ps: Concert Band\, Ceremonial Marching Band\, Commanders Jazz Ensemble\, M
 obility popular music ensemble\, Travis Brass quintet\, Golden West Brass 
 ensemble\, Golden West Winds woodwind quintet\, Sierra Winds woodwind ense
 mble\, Clarinet Quartet\, and Galaxy popular music ensemble. Additionally\
 , the Band of the Golden West provides solo vocalists for the performance 
 of our National Anthem and solo buglers for Taps details.\n\nThe Band of t
 he Golden West has performed for gubernatorial inaugurations\, with guest 
 artists such as Marvin Stamm\, Arturo Sandoval and Bob Hope\, and for the 
 fans of Oakland Athletics\, San Francisco Giants\, Oakland Raiders\, San F
 rancisco 49ers\, and Infineon NASCAR races. In the last ten years the band
  has led off three Tournament of Roses parades in Pasadena\, CA. In 2004\,
  the USAF Band of the Golden West was honored to support the interment cer
 emony of President Ronald Reagan in Simi Valley\, CA\, an event\, viewed b
 y a global TV audience of over 400 million. More recent events include fea
 tured performances with the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, San Francisco Symph
 ony\, the Hollywood Bowl Orchestra and Pentatonix.\n\n**With gratitude to 
 our sponsor\, Eddinger Enterprises\, Inc.**\n\n**Tuesday\, September 22nd\
 , at 7:30 PM.** Doors open at 6:30 PM.\n\n**Please email us if you need wh
 eelchair accessible spaces so we can reserve it: events@raventheater.org. 
 For people using walkers\, the back row is most suitable as it has more le
 g room.**
LOCATION:The Raven Performing Arts Theater
URL:https://raventheater.org/events/usaf-commanders-jazz-ensemble/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260922T170000
UID:260906Oeioi3DLfUuXa-py8JvaLw
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
