BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Culinary Student Lunch Pop-UP
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T130000
UID:2609110aKbkaoqq0yqW0PNs70wpA
DESCRIPTION:Our Culinary Student Pop-Up Lunch Cafe is back! There will be 
 two lunch service windows: Wednesday through Friday on September 16th-18t
 h and again 23rd-25th\, 11:30 AM to 1 PM. This tradition marks the culmina
 tion of our current adult culinary job training cohort in Petaluma\, with 
 every dish on the menu inspired and shaped by the students themselves. It'
 s a chance for our trainees to put their new skills to the test working th
 e line in a real service setting - and for you to taste what they've been 
 working on and cheer them on as they graduate into their next chapter.\n\n
 Consider treating your whole team or company to lunch those weeks - it's a
  great way to support our students while feeding your crew! There’s limi
 ted space to eat onsite\, so please plan to pick up\, take it with you\, a
 nd enjoy.\n\nHead to sonomafamilymeal.org for the full menu and option t
 o place your order online in advance.
LOCATION:Sonoma Family Meal Community Kitchen
URL:http://sonomafamilymeal.org
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Stoneteeth & Lindsay Gang
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T223000
UID:260909y67CKtw-Dkq1w1mb85twAQ
DESCRIPTION:$15 General Admission\n\nDoors at 7:30pm.\n\nShow from 8:15-10
 :30pm with one intermission\n\nEarlybirds!\n\n$3 off each ticket when you 
 use the code earlybird12 at checkout. Offer ends September 20th.\n\nPro
 mo Codes are not applicable at the door.\n\nCodes are not case sensitiv
 e\, but must be typed as one word.\n\nMike Clark\, a singer songwriter fro
 m Colorado best known for his band Mike Clark and the Sugar Sounds was rea
 dy for a new challenge and direction when he started penning the words and
  concepts for what would become Stoneteeth.    Cole Halvorsen\, an audio
  engineer and musician from Reno\, Nevada had recently returned home to Re
 no\, NV after spending the last four years mastering his craft working wit
 h legendary Producer/Engineer Tucker Martine at Flora Recording & Playback
  in Portland Oregon.  In the summer of 2024\, the two met by chance as Ha
 lvorsen had been scheduled last minute to fill in as recording engineer on
  the most recent Sugar Sounds album \\(Hot Coffee\\). The two hit it off i
 mmediately and it became obvious that this was going to be an unstoppable 
 collaborative duo. They met up in June of ‘25 in Halvorsen’s home stud
 io with no idea of what was going to be made. Mike had lyrics written\, ve
 ry rough rhythmic ideas in beatbox form and a concept for a down on his lu
 ck character named Stoneteeth\; derived from Clarks own life experiences a
 nd distaste for the wealthy elite and political profiteers.     From the
  drop of the first note as Cole crafted the beats  that would become the 
 building blocks of an amazing album together they crafted track after trac
 k sometimes working through the night into the morning.   It was boozy an
 d loose\, inspired and real with both men adding multiple instruments and 
 stacking fat harmonies from the deep guttural growls to sultry falsettos. 
  The pace was alive and inspired and the results are amazing.  Stoneteet
 h is born.   Now they head out into the world to perform these songs with
  several album release shows already in the works for 2026. Stoneteeth is 
 a voice from the bottom\, the gutter\, the last penny in your pocket\, the
  drunk sleeping at the end of the bar full of wisdom and cheap whiskey tha
 t you write off until they wake up and piss on your party.  \n\nhttps://w
 ww.stoneteethband.com/ https://www.instagram.com/stoneteethband/ https://y
 outu.be/UsKIpGeJJc8?si=ib1KzADfuAZogTbg\n\nSinger-songwriter\, Lindsay Ga
 ng\, was born and raised in Northern California. The traveling songbird's 
 experiences have taken her from the West Coast to the Midwest\, the East C
 oast\, and back. She makes sense of the ebb and flow of life by turning he
 r experiences and travels into song. With warm vocals\, lush guitar\, and 
 tender\, heartfelt lyrics\, her songs possess the quality of exceptional f
 idelity\; they are reflective\, intimate\, and raw - echoing indie folk wi
 th a dreamy Americana feel. Singing with vulnerability and softness\, she 
 has been said to evoke a sense of comfort that makes a listener feel at ho
 me. Her songwriting has been described as deeply personal\, yet approachab
 le and relatable. Her music has been compared to the greats such as Joan B
 aez and Joni Mitchell\, but "..imbued with modern sensibilities of modern-
 day performers such as Angel Olsen or Gregory Alan Isakov".\n\nhttps://www
 .instagram.com/lindsaygangmusic https://www.facebook.com/lindsaygangmusic 
 https://www.youtube.com/@lindsaygang https://lindsaygang.bandcamp.com/\n\n
 Recommended If You Like: Stoneteenth: Hillstomp\, Tom Waits\, Screamin Jay
  Hawkins\, RL Burnside Lindsay Gang: Joan Baez and Joni Mitchell
LOCATION:The Lost Church
URL:https://thelostchurch.my.salesforce-sites.com/ticket#/instances/a0FUh0
 0000CGnsHMAT
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T170000
UID:2609065tIc2NT6qEeuT99qxdl4bQ
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
