BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bye Bye Birdie
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T160000
UID:2609102NOjWyYDhkisIc6NdzHrKg
DESCRIPTION:A joyful\, high-energy musical comedy packed with unforgettabl
 e songs\, humor\, and heart\, "Bye Bye Birdie" is a celebration of youth\,
  fame\, and the delightful chaos that happens when pop culture collides wi
 th small-town life.\n\nFrom the high-energy “The Telephone Hour” to th
 e show-stopping “A Lot of Livin’ to Do\,” the heartfelt “How Lovel
 y to Be a Woman\,” and the iconic “Put On a Happy Face\,” this produ
 ction is filled with classic musical theater moments the whole family can 
 enjoy.\n\nCinnabar Theater's production is directed by Zachary Hasbany\, w
 ith book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, and lyrics by Lee
  Adams\, originally produced by Edward Padula. The show is presented throu
 gh special arrangement with Concord Theatricals.\n\n**Show details**\n\nCr
 eated by: Book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, lyrics by L
 ee Adams\n\nDirector: Zachary Hasbany\n\nLocation: Sonoma State University
  – Warren Theater\, Ives Hall\, 1801 East Cotati Ave\, Rohnert Park\, CA
  94928\n\nPerformance dates: September 18 – October 4\, 2026\; Fridays &
  Saturdays 7:30pm\, Sundays 2:00pm\n\nEstimated run time: 2 hours\, with o
 ne 10-minute intermission\n\nRecommended for families / all ages\n\n**ASL-
 Interpreted Performance**\n\nSunday\, September 27 at 2:00 PM\n\nThis perf
 ormance will feature American Sign Language (ASL) interpretation. Patrons 
 who would like seating with a clear view of the ASL interpreter can contac
 t the Box Office at (707) 763-8920 or boxoffice@cinnabartheater.org.\n\nTi
 ckets: https://ci.ovationtix.com/35211
LOCATION:Sonoma State University - Warren Auditorium
URL:https://cinnabartheater.org/show/byebyebirdie/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Healdsburg Internation Short Film Festival 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T223000
UID:260909tKWYk-QqLUCsvrXNqkv7iQ
DESCRIPTION:Set in the heart of California wine country\, the highly antic
 ipated Healdsburg International Short Film Festival (HISFF) returns this S
 eptember for its 5th year\, promising another weekend of unforgettable ci
 nema\, parties\, and a red carpet opening night. This three-day festival r
 uns from September 25th through September 27th. Organized in collaboratio
 n with the **Healdsburg Center for the Arts (HCA**)\, HISFF 2026 coincide
 s with HCA’s **Festival of the Arts**\, creating a vibrant\, art-fille
 d weekend.\n\nHISFF 2026 kicks off with an Opening Night Soirée\, a livel
 y red-carpet arrival and photo op at the Raven\, and an exquisite collect
 ion of mixed genre short films. Festivities continue with multiple screeni
 ng sessions\, a Saturday night party at **The Rooftop at the Harmon Guest
  House**\, and a filmmaker breakfast\, culminating in an awards ceremony o
 n Sunday night.\n\nOur panel of notable judges from the entertainment indu
 stry makes the HISFF an attraction for new and veteran filmmakers alike 
 — people like **Ed Begley\, Jr.\, Laraine Newman\, Billy Ray\, Tom Waits
 **\, and so many more. Several judges are attending the festival this year
 \, including writer Billy Ray (*The Hunger Games* and the soon-to-be rele
 ased *The Hunger Games: Sunrise On the Reaping* )\, who will make a book
 -signing appearance at the Raven on Saturday\, September 26th (Time TBD).\
 n\nVisit our booth at the Healdsburg Festival of the Arts on Saturday\, S
 eptember 26th\, from 10 am to 6 pm on Center Street in front of HCA (just
  steps away from the Raven). The street will be lined with booths featurin
 g fine arts\, crafts\, jewelry\, poetry\, live music\, food trucks\, and m
 ore!\n\nCopperfield’s is organizing a free-admission book-signing event 
 with screenwriter (and festival jurist) Billy Ray at the Raven from 11:00 
 am\, also on Saturday\, September 26th. Books will be available for purcha
 se and signing\, but it’s best to order your copy ahead of time from Cop
 perfield’s.\n\n**All Access Pass: $175. Includes:**\n\n\\- Red-Carpet ar
 rival and photo op begins at 6 pm at the Raven.\n\n\\- Opening Night Kick-
 Off Screening Block\, 7:00 pm.\n\n\\- Opening Night Kick-Off Soirée\, 8:3
 0 pm at Portalupi Wines.\n\n\\- Saturday Night Sweet & Uncorked Social at 
 the beautiful Harmon House Rooftop\, 8:15 pm.\n\n\\- Every screening sessi
 on\, including the filmmaker Q&A sessions.
LOCATION:The Raven Performing Arts Theater
URL:https://raventheater.org/events/hisff2026/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:No Cell Sundays: Marcus & Devin
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T143000
UID:260909gjRiffY6SU2DqziiUsF6bA
DESCRIPTION:Soul Meets Keys: Marcus & Devin Live\n\nJoin us for No Cell Su
 ndays with Marcus & Devin\, a duo that transforms Sunday afternoons into s
 omething extraordinary.\n\nMarcus Urani is one of Northern California's mo
 st celebrated pianists and keyboardists. Educated in musical theory and an
  original member of Groundation\, Marcus has toured the world and spent 25
 \\+ years composing\, arranging\, producing\, and playing with countless m
 usical acts. With mastery across nearly all genres\, Marcus creates rich m
 usical landscapes that captivate from the first note.\n\nPaired with vocal
 ist and lyricist Devin Ruddick's emotive and soaring vocals\, this duo del
 ivers a one-two punch that captivates without intrusion—adding melodic b
 liss as they cover a catalog of meaningful artists spanning seven decades.
 \n\nWhat's No Cell Sundays?\n\nIt's simple: disconnect from your devices\,
  reconnect with great wine\, live music\, and the people around you. Check
  your phone with us at the door and receive a complimentary tasting. Bring
  a blanket\, grab a glass\, and spend your Sunday the way it was meant to 
 be spent.
LOCATION:Hook & Ladder Winery
URL:https://www.hookandladderwinery.com/event/no-cell-sundays-marcus-devin
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T170000
UID:260906pO9J-BW9CUecr9zgzaauKg
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
