BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260930T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260930T170000
UID:260906wEcJruhkQ0qK7f79nIMsCw
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
