BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reception and Art Sale: California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T190000
UID:26090907xmaZZcwkWBuNMT1myvsg
DESCRIPTION:**September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Friday\
 , Oct. 2\, from 5-7 p.m.\, the public is invited to the 6th California Nat
 ive Creations Art Show artists' reception and art sale\, which features th
 e Tri-County Pomo Dancers.**\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native a
 rtists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in thei
 r art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The 
 artists showing or selling work include:\n\n·         Meyo Marruf
 o\, painter\, guest curator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley 
 Art Museum\,\n\n·         Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood
  Valley Rancheria\, cultural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·   
       Henry Edward Frank\, *Yurok/Kashaya Pomo*\, who will show paint
 ings and work with Legos titled *Mind Prison\,*\n\n·         Digi
 tal artist Nicolas (Nico) Vargas\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\, whose a
 rt was featured at the 55th Annual Stanford Powwow\,\n\n\\* plus Gloria Ca
 rretero\; Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaR
 ose\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Angelica Smith\,*Manchester Point Ar
 ena and Yokayo Rancheria*\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morga
 n\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenb
 augh\; Katie Williams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, *Big Valle
 y Pomo Tribe*.\n\n“We are not just people of the past\,” says Rose Ham
 mock (*Pomo\, Wailacki\, & Maidu*\, *Tribal Member Round Valley Indian Tri
 bes)*\, community leader\, native culture teacher and Pomo Project Board M
 ember. “There is great importance in highlighting Native Artists in publ
 ic spaces. For so long\, our communities and their many talents have gone 
 unnoticed and unrecognized - today that narrative has shifted. Now there a
 re Native Art Shows\, Fashion Shows\, and Art Showcases highlighting art a
 nd basketry."\n\nSays digital artist and native dancer Nicholas Vargas\, 
 “To me\, my art is a living archive\, a digital space where I reclaim ou
 r narrative and honor the resilience of my people.”\n\nPainter and bead 
 worker Gloria Carretero (*Pinoleville Pomo Nation Tribe*) adds\, **“**I 
 wish to honor my ancestors with native-inspired art.”
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
UID:260906VwGgM7InvUqzXLxoxfph8Q
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fiber\, Painting and Prints Gallery Opening Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T190000
UID:260906R20Ho4v7VEGjBdJuIdwcYQ
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a special gallery exhibition featuring the artwork
  of Tricia O’Brien and Lori Goldman\, showcasing a beautiful collection 
 of fiber\, paintings\, and prints at Art Escape.\n\nThe opening reception 
 will also include a free mini tapestry workshop\, where you’ll have the 
 chance to get creative and make your own small tapestry using stamp eraser
 s\, ink pads\, and sticks. No experience is necessary — just come ready 
 to experiment\, play\, and make something!\n\nTricia O’Brien’s work ex
 plores the intersection of abstract and organic imagery through printmakin
 g\, often incorporating recyclable and discarded materials to create her u
 nique\, one-of-a-kind monoprints. Her work reflects a fascinating balance 
 between traditional printmaking processes and unexpected textures\, markin
 gs\, and materials.\n\nCome enjoy the exhibition\, meet fellow art lovers\
 , and try your hand at a fun and approachable fiber art project. Everyone 
 is welcome!
LOCATION:Art Escape Sonoma
URL:https://www.artescapesonoma.com/gallery-exhibits/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
