BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Work In Progress - Robert Reich new Movie
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T201500
UID:260912-HkoZRsMhk6VBH5TsKT5SA
DESCRIPTION:&ldquo\;The Work in Progress&rdquo\; takes viewers on a tour o
 f the American spirit at a time of deep division. Guided by UC Berkeley em
 eritus scholar Robert Reich\, the documentary finds tension and doubt ever
 ywhere &mdash\; but also signs of hope.Inspired by Robert Reich's curiosit
 y and humanity\, it is a testament to the power of listening\, civic engag
 ement\, and the belief that our shared future is still ours to shape.  Th 
 film begins with Professor Reich asking "What do we owe each other?"  What
  follows is a journey to find out. \n\nA Q&amp\;A with producers Heather K
 inlaw Lofthouse\, a UC Berkeley graduate\, and Elliot Kirschner will follo
 w the film\n\nThe film is a timely and thought-provoking addition to Reich
 's catalogue of writing\, film\, media appearances and other public commen
 tary. Perhaps it's no surprise that it emerges from the culture of UC Berk
 eley. Scholars and students in recent years have pioneered the practice of
  &ldquo\;bridging&rdquo\; profound political divisions through open-minded
  conversation\; one Berkeley course helps students practice how to be open
  to opposing views.\n\nReich's core idea was not to bring together liberal
 s and conservatives\, but to convene younger and older Americans &mdash\; 
 over 100 of them in all\, many living in the American heartland.Strangers 
 aged 30 to 80 were brought together in slices of our vast landscape. They 
 were presented with 50 questions. For example: What do we owe each other? 
 What are you most proud of? What one word would you use to describe Americ
 a? Etc. And they were asked to talk. One person would answer and the other
 \\(s\\) would\, in Reich's words\, &ldquo\;eloquently listen.&rdquo\;\n\n&
 ldquo\;With the age divide\,&rdquo\; Reich explains in the film\, &ldquo\;
 you've got a lot of people who are 30\, and they are the future\, and a lo
 t of people who are boomers\, like me. We are the past.&rdquo\;\n\nTrying 
 to compress a complex project into a tight timeline\, the filmmakers made 
 strategic decisions that may seem counterintuitive in an era of television
  shouting and podcast snark.\n\nWe all want to be seen and see one another
 . We all want to be included. And the more we acknowledge that\, the faste
 r our curiosity will take over and the kind of fracture we have right now 
 will dissolve into the exciting mosaic of the amazingly diverse population
  displayed in this film.\n\nIt is not commonality we need\; it is curiosit
 y!\n\nDoors open at 5:15 for 6:00 movie. The movie is 106 minutes long. Pa
 nel with producers Heather Kinlaw Lofthouse\, a UC Berkeley graduate\, and
  Elliot Kirschner will follow the film
LOCATION:Sebastiani Theatre
URL:https://mynorthbaytickets.ticketsauce.com/e/work-in-progress-robert-re
 ich-new-movie?aff=cityspark
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
